Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to build a
Python command-line tool with the following specification: Build a Python 3
command-line utility that strips comments from Bash/shell scripts while
preserving the scripts' runtime behavior, using a tree-sitter-based parser for
accurate comment detection.
Make the script self-contained in a single file with an executable shebang line
for Termux on Android (`#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`), and
include `from __future__ import annotations` at the top. **Dependencies:** Use
the standard library modules `argparse`, `multiprocessing`, `os`, `re`, `sys`,
and `pathlib.Path`, plus `typing.TYPE_CHECKING` (importing
`collections.abc.Generator` and `Iterable` only under that guard).
Third-party dependencies are `tree_sitter_bash` (which provides `language()`)
and `tree_sitter` (providing `Language`, `Node`, and `Parser`).
Construct a module-level Bash `Language` from `tree_sitter_bash.language()` and
a module-level `Parser` bound to it, with type annotations. **Module-level
constants:** - `SHEBANG_PREFIXES`: a tuple of byte-string shebang prefixes
recognized as shell scripts, covering `/bin/bash`, `/bin/sh`, `/usr/bin/env
bash`, `/usr/bin/env sh`, `/bin/env bash`, `/bin/env sh`, `/usr/bin/env zsh`,
and `/bin/zsh`. - A compiled regex operating on bytes that matches runs of three
or more blank lines (lines containing only spaces/tabs followed by newline),
used to collapse excess blank lines after comment removal. **Function
`is_bash_file(path: Path) -> bool`:** Return True if the file suffix
(case-insensitive) is `.sh` or `.bash`.
Otherwise open the file in binary mode, read its first line, and return True if
it starts with any shebang prefix.
On `OSError` while opening or reading, return False. **Function
`find_comment_ranges(source: bytes) -> list[tuple[int, int, bool]]`:** Parse the
source bytes with the tree-sitter parser and walk the syntax tree recursively to
find nodes of type `comment`.
Skip a comment that begins at byte 0 when the source starts with `#!` (preserve
the shebang).
For each retained comment, determine the start of its line (scanning backward
for a newline byte) and whether the comment is the only non-whitespace content
on its line, returning each as a tuple of (start byte, end byte, whether it is a
full-line comment).
Also include the description of further logic: the rest of the walk recurses
into child nodes.
Preserve ordering of ranges as encountered. **Expected remaining behavior (to
implement fully):** - A function to strip comments from source bytes: remove
each identified comment range; for full-line comments remove the whole line
including its trailing newline; for inline comments remove only the comment text
and any whitespace immediately preceding it on that line.
Apply removals from the end of the file backward so offsets remain valid.
Then collapse runs of three or more blank lines using the blank-run regex into a
single blank line, and ensure the output keeps the shebang intact. - A per-file
processing function that reads the file bytes, checks whether it is a Bash file,
strips comments, and writes the result back only if content changed.
Support a dry-run mode that reports without writing, and a mode that writes to a
separate output path or prints a summary.
Handle `OSError` and tree-sitter parse issues per file by reporting an error to
stderr and continuing; never abort the whole run because one file failed. - A
file discovery generator that accepts paths: for a file, yield it if it is a
bash file; for a directory, recursively walk it (using `os.walk` or
`Path.rglob`), skip hidden directories and common non-source directories such as
`.git`, and yield bash files only.
Skip nonexistent paths with a warning to stderr. - A `main()` function using
`argparse` with positional paths (one or more), options for dry-run (`--dry-run`
/ `-n`), a verbose flag (`--verbose` / `-v`), and a jobs count option (`--jobs`
/ `-j`, default to CPU count via `os.cpu_count()`), using `multiprocessing.Pool`
(or `ProcessPoolExecutor`-style logic) to process files in parallel, with
`jobs=1` running sequentially in-process.
Print a final summary line with counts of files scanned, modified, and errors.
Return exit code 0 on success, 1 if any errors occurred, and 2 for usage
problems. - Guard the entry point with `if __name__ == "__main__":` and call
`sys.exit(main())`. **Execution and usage:** Run as `python script.py [options]
PATH...`.
Requires `pip install tree-sitter tree-sitter-bash`.
Designed to run under Termux; file operations use binary I/O throughout.
Use `from __future__ import annotations` for postponed annotations. **Edge cases
to handle:** empty files, files with only a shebang, files with no comments
(leave unchanged, do not rewrite), comments inside strings (must not be removed,
relying on the parser to distinguish comment nodes), trailing whitespace before
inline comments, CRLF line endings preserved where possible, unreadable files,
and non-UTF-8 content (operate on bytes, never decode).
Produce clean, typed, idiomatic Python with docstrings where helpful, consistent
with the style of the surrounding code. ---
